p.src:
Electromagnetic radiation forms a continuous spectrum. Ordered from longer to shorter wavelength, the conventional names are $\begin{aligned}
\text{radio}
&\longrightarrow \text{microwave}
\longrightarrow \text{infrared}
\longrightarrow \text{visible}\\
&\longrightarrow \text{ultraviolet}
\longrightarrow \text{X ray}
\longrightarrow \text{gamma ray}.
\end{aligned}$ Visible light occupies only a narrow interval. The boundaries between the named regions are conventional, so no exact dividing wavelengths are needed here. Radio waves may be extremely long; gamma radiation is the very-short-wavelength end of the electromagnetic spectrum. The difficulty is now sharp: gamma radiation is classically a continuous field wave, yet it is detected in discrete blips.

